/target
# `/target` above only anchors to the repo root — examples/rust/,
# sdks/rust/, deploy/backup-tool/, and ci/e2e-integration/ are their own
# separate Cargo workspaces (see each one's own Cargo.toml), so their
# build output needs its own entry.
/examples/rust/target
/sdks/rust/target
/deploy/backup-tool/target
/ci/e2e-integration/target
sdks/node/*/dist/
**/*.rs.bk
# Cargo.lock is committed. This workspace ships binaries (sms-api, sms-worker)
# against a pre-1.0 framework pinned to =0.4.16; a floating lock file would
# quietly undo that pin on every fresh checkout.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
/secrets/
# deploy/secrets/ holds real, host-specific credentials at runtime
# (console-private-key.pem, console-client-id, rclone.conf — see
# docs/runbooks/deployment.adoc's "Secrets" section) — `*.pem`/`*.key`
# above already cover the private key, but a plain-text file like
# rclone.conf or a client id needs its own rule, and `/secrets/` above
# only anchors to the repo root, not `deploy/secrets/`. Ignore everything
# in the directory except the two example/placeholder files this repo
# ships, so `deploy/secrets/` itself still exists (and is browsable) in a
# fresh clone without any real secret ever being committed by accident.
deploy/secrets/*
!deploy/secrets/.gitkeep
!deploy/secrets/rclone.conf.example
.DS_Store
node_modules/
/pgdata
.next/
.turbo/
# `just e2e-integration`'s own scratch state — the second, "external
# integrator" client's private key/id, extracted from `compose.dev.yaml`'s
# named secrets volume onto the host so `ci/e2e-integration` (running
# in-network) and this recipe's own `docker compose cp` have somewhere to
# put them. Never committed; regenerated fresh on every run.
/.e2e/
# `just demo`'s own scratch state — pepper, provisioned key, pidfiles,
# process logs. Never committed; regenerated fresh on every `just demo`.
/.demo/
frontends/apps/admin/public/sw*.js
*.tsbuildinfo

# frontends/packages/sms-client (T3) is entirely generated by
# `cratestack generate-typescript` from schemas/vsms.cstack — per the
# owner's standing rule, auto-generated code is never committed. `just
# client-gen` (or CI's own generation step; see .github/workflows/ci.yml's
# `js` job) writes it fresh every time, before anything that consumes it.
#
# The ONE exception is `package.json`, tracked deliberately: pnpm needs a
# package.json on disk to recognise this as a workspace member and to
# validate `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` against the committed
# pnpm-lock.yaml — and that install has to run BEFORE generation is
# possible (generation itself needs no node_modules, but everything
# downstream of `pnpm install` — typecheck, build — needs the workspace
# resolved first). The tracked package.json is not hand-maintained: it is
# exactly what generation itself writes (the generator's dependency
# list is schema-invariant boilerplate, not derived from model content),
# so committing it commits generation's own output, not a fork of it.
# Regeneration overwrites it with identical bytes on every run; if a
# `cratestack` upgrade ever changes that template's shape, regenerate and
# recommit this one file. See frontends/packages/sms-client/GENERATING.md.
frontends/packages/sms-client/*
!frontends/packages/sms-client/package.json
!frontends/packages/sms-client/GENERATING.md

# deploy/charts/vsms's `common` dependency, fetched by `helm dependency
# build` (release.yml runs this before packaging) from Chart.lock — same
# "generated, never committed" convention as frontends/packages/sms-client above.
# Chart.lock itself IS committed (it's the pin, same role Cargo.lock
# plays for the Rust workspace).
deploy/charts/vsms/charts/
